  "summary": "The elegant, minimalist staging aesthetic of Kunqu opera, China's oldest continuously performed theatrical form.",
  "description": "Kunqu features spare, poetic settings with simple props and gestures that suggest rather than represent, using subtle movements and poetic language to convey complex emotions. This refined aesthetic developed during the Ming Dynasty as a reaction against the more elaborate forms of opera, emphasizing literary quality and emotional subtlety through minimalist staging.",
  "culturalContext": "Kunqu refers to Kunshan tune (昆山腔, Kūnshān qiāng), a repertory of songs and performances from Kunshan in Suzhou. The Kunshan tune is generally believed to have been developed during the Ming dynasty by Wei Liangfu (魏良輔), who was from the port of Taicang. A more recent discovery, however, suggests that Kunshan tune was introduced in the late Yuan dynasty by Gu Jian (顾坚), who developed an early form of Kunqu with a group of musicians in Kunshan. Nevertheless, Wei is believed to have modified Kunshan tune with songs of Haiyan (海鹽) near Hangzhou and Yiyang (弋陽) of Jiangxi; he also combined nanxi rhythms, which often used flute, with the northern zaju style, where plucked string instruments were preferred. The resultant elegant Kunshan tunes are often called \"water mill\" tunes (水磨調, shuimo diao).",
